==============================================================================
7. (A) LOGICAL STATUS AND TRIVIAL BOUNDS
==============================================================================
  (A) S(x) = o(x) is a limit statement.  A finite computation yields at best
      |S(x)| <= M(x0) at the finitely many computed x <= x0 (M(10^8) = 6454),
      and by A1 that same data is equally consistent with S(x) ~ x.  Hence:
        * NO finite computation can PROVE o(x);
        * a finite computation CAN REFUTE it, by exhibiting one x with
          |S(x)| >= c*x, c > 0 fixed.  No such x occurs here: the largest
          |S(x)|/x over x <= 10^8 is 3.000e-02 (at x=10^3, for x=10^8 it is 4.632e-05),
          so the computation finds no refutation -- and cannot.
[PASS] A2 :: trivial bound |sum_{n<=x} lambda(n)lambda(n+1)| <= x-2 by direct enumeration for every x in [2,10^7] (tol=0)
        violations: x in [1] ; x=1 is the degenerate exception (x-2 = -1 < |S(1)| = 1).
        max |sum_{n<=x} lambda(n)lambda(n+1)| = 2716 at x = 9754862, i.e. |.|/x = 2.784e-04: the trivial term-count bound |.| <= x is never approached.
[PASS] A3 :: (A) status: S(x) = o(x) is OPEN; the round's refusal to claim a proof is CORRECT and is exactly what A1 demonstrates
        no computation, however far, changes this; the only unconditional content audited here is the logarithmic average (C)

